          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lagerlof-gosta-berlings-saga-61d2fc5156f37da4\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Anna’s embedded “jag älskar dig” presents her continuing love for Gösta as true and as the condition grounding her promise to love his friends. The commitment expressed by the complete sentence concerns the friends, not an undertaking to love Gösta. Nothing indicates that the highlighted words are a reflex-like affective discharge rather than a composed avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The clause is temporally conditional (“as long as”), not a standalone assertion, but it still clearly presents Anna’s present and continuing love as real.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Anna directly addresses Gösta; the highlighted equivalent occurs as an embedded temporal-condition clause within her spoken reply: “As long as I love you, I shall love them.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Gösta asks Anna to remain with and not hate his friends as part of their mutual renunciation. Her reply makes her love for him the motive and temporal condition for loving them. Her mournful smile and the scene’s emotional intensity frame a deliberate, self-sacrificial response, but do not establish expressive/reflexive force in the highlighted words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Så länge jag älskar dig, skall jag älska dem.” (“As long as I love you, I shall love them.”)\",\"supports\":\"The highlighted clause presents Anna’s love for Gösta as a continuing state, strongly supporting T; the future-oriented undertaking in the full sentence has loving Gösta as its condition rather than its object.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Gösta asks, “Och inte hata mina fattiga vänner?” immediately before Anna’s reply.\",\"supports\":\"Shows that the sentence’s practical commitment concerns Gösta’s friends, limiting support for P in the core “I love you” clause itself.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Hon log vemodigt” (“She smiled mournfully”) before speaking, after agreeing to sacrifice herself and remain.\",\"supports\":\"Frames the utterance as emotionally laden but controlled and responsive; it provides no positive evidence of reflex-like verbal discharge, so E remains unsupported.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as Swedish present-tense “jag älskar dig” within a main-or-embedded construction and reviewed as a valid explicit I-love-you instance.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the target is an explicit love predication despite its embedding under “så länge.”\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the highlighted clause’s avowal of an enduring emotional state. The surrounding promise and emotional delivery can be described contextually without assigning P or E to the core clause, and no residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
